# Load the data into Pandas
df = pd.DataFrame(records)

# Apply a function to a column
- First Approach:
df['Column_Name'] = df['Column_Name'].apply(function)
- Second Approach:
df['Column_Name'] = function(df['Column_Name'])

example:
1. df['Name'] = df['Name'].apply(str.upper) or df['Name'] = df['Name'].apply(lambda x: x.lower())
2. df['Name'] = df['Name'].str.upper()

# Rename a Column
df = df.rename(columns={'Old_Column_Name': 'New_Column_Name'})

# Create a new Pandas DataFrame 
newdf = df.copy(deep=True)

# Reset the Indexes
df = df.reset_index(drop=True)
> drop=True: This argument means that the old index will not be added as a column in the DataFrame. Without drop=True, the original index would be added as a new column.

# Filter the DataFrame
filterdf = df[df['Column_Name'] == filter_value]

# Remove NA values
df = df.dropna()
> axis=0 (default): Drop rows with missing values.
> axis=1: Drop columns with missing values.
> subset=[column1, column2]: Only check specific columns for missing values.
> how='any' (default): Drop rows or columns with any missing value.
> how='all': Drop rows or columns where all values are missing.

# fill missing values NA with 0
df['Column_Name'] = df['Column_Name'].fillna(0)

# Merge Dataframes
pd.merge(left, right, how='inner', on=None, left_on=None, right_on=None, left_index=False, right_index=False, suffixes=('_x', '_y'), sort=False)
Parameters:
> left: The first DataFrame to merge.
> right: The second DataFrame to merge.
> how: The type of join to perform:
  > 'inner': Only rows with keys that appear in both DataFrames (default).
  > 'left': All rows from the left DataFrame, matching rows from the right DataFrame.
  > 'right': All rows from the right DataFrame, matching rows from the left DataFrame.
  > 'outer': All rows from both DataFrames, with NaN where there are no matches.
> on: The column(s) to join on (must be present in both DataFrames).
> left_on: Column(s) from the left DataFrame to join on.
> right_on: Column(s) from the right DataFrame to join on.
> left_index: If True, uses the index of the left DataFrame for merging.
> right_index: If True, uses the index of the right DataFrame for merging.
> suffixes: A tuple specifying suffixes to append to columns with the same name in both DataFrames (default is ('_x', '_y')).
> sort: If True, sorts the result DataFrame by the join keys (default is False).

Common Use Cases:
Merge on a Single Column (common column in both DataFrames):
  merged_df = pd.merge(df1, df2, on='column_name', how='inner')
Merge on Different Column Names:
  merged_df = pd.merge(df1, df2, left_on='left_column', right_on='right_column', how='outer')
Merge Using Index:
  merged_df = pd.merge(df1, df2, left_index=True, right_index=True, how='left')



